# Project

A peer interview practice tool for designers. Two people book one session, take turns as interviewer and candidate on a shared whiteboard, and both get an AI report afterwards.

Full spec in `docs/prd.md`. Build order in `docs/build-phases.md`. Read both before suggesting anything.

# How we work

- Build strictly one phase at a time, in the order given in build-phases.md.
- Never scaffold ahead. No auth, no database, no video, no matching until their phase arrives.
- Smallest working version first. I will ask for refinements after I have tried it.
- If something seems missing, ask before adding it. Do not fill gaps by guessing.
- When I describe a bug, ask to see the behaviour before rewriting code.

# Stack

- Next.js, App Router, TypeScript
- Tailwind
- Excalidraw, embedded, MIT licensed. Do not suggest tldraw, it needs a paid licence key in production.
- Claude API for hidden brief answers and post-session reports
- Postgres via Supabase, from phase 8 only
- LiveKit or Daily for video, from phase 6 only
- excalidraw-room for canvas sync, from phase 6 only

# Code conventions

- Functional components, hooks, no class components
- Keep prompts, hidden briefs, fact lists and rubrics in config files, not inline in components
- Server-side anything that touches an API key. Never expose keys client-side.
- Store all times in UTC
- Prefer a few clear files over many tiny ones

# Git

I am learning the git workflow on this project. So:

- Explain any git command before running it, and prefer that I run it myself.
- Do not commit, push, merge or create branches unless I ask.
- One branch per phase, named like `phase-1-whiteboard-shell`.
- Never commit directly to `main`.

# Product rules that are easy to get wrong

- The AI is silent during a session. It feeds cards to the interviewer's rail and writes the report afterwards. It never speaks to the candidate mid-round.
- Candidate screen: full width video, two square cards at the bottom for Whiteboard and Notes, thin countdown overlay. Nothing else. Do not add controls for visual balance.
- The whiteboard opens automatically at session start and the card toggles back to full video. It never starts hidden.
- Notes are private to the candidate and never shown to the peer.
- Interviewer rail is 30 percent, two tabs: Prompt and Evaluation. Cards pop over the rail regardless of the active tab and are dismissible. Nothing urgent should require a tab switch.
- The hidden brief is revealed only in response to questions the candidate actually asks.
- Do not grade the drawing itself. Canvas snapshots answer one narrow question: does the canvas match what they said they were doing.
- The canvas is embedded. There is no screen sharing anywhere in this product.
- Desktop only. Do not spend effort on mobile layouts.